{"text": "Category P is Gifted. The educational description is: A student with demonstrated or potential exceptionally high capability in intellect, creativity, or a specific discipline. Gifted learners are diverse and may also have disabilities or uneven development. This description is about educational impact and the support required for meaningful participation. It should not be used as a prediction of intelligence, personality, or future potential.\n\nOur fictional student is Daniel Brown, who is in Grade 7 and uses he/him pronouns. Daniel demonstrates exceptional mathematical reasoning and creative problem-solving. He learns rapidly, needs greater depth and complexity, and experiences perfectionism when tasks feel evaluative. This background information matters because it connects the designation to observable access needs at school.\n\nWhen we plan for Daniel, we ask three practical questions: What can the student already do? What barriers are interfering with participation or learning? And what support will remove those barriers while building independence? Those questions keep the profile individualized and useful. Planning should include the student, family, teacher, case manager, and relevant specialists.\n\nI will now hand the presentation to Presenter 2, who will begin with Daniel’s strengths and communication.", "voice": "en-GB-SoniaNeural", "audio": "special-education-P-02.mp3", "words": [{"text": "Category", "start": 0.1, "end": 0.7, "start_char": 0, "end_char": 8}, {"text": "P", "start": 0.7125, "end": 1.075, "start_char": 9, "end_char": 10}, {"text": "is", "start": 1.0875, "end": 1.3, "start_char": 11, "end_char": 13}, {"text": "Gifted", "start": 1.3125, "end": 1.925, "start_char": 14, "end_char": 20}, {"text": "The", "start": 2.325, "end": 2.45, "start_char": 22, "end_char": 25}, {"text": "educational", "start": 2.4625, "end": 3.075, "start_char": 26, "end_char": 37}, {"text": "description", "start": 3.0875, "end": 3.6375, "start_char": 38, "end_c